@title Incident Report: Freezer 3 Temperature Excursion
@subtitle Compressor failure and delayed alarm response, Thursday 2026-10-15
@date 2026-10-15
@width wide

## Summary

Freezer 3 was above its -18.0 C limit for 3 hours 25 minutes, from 04:05 to 07:30. Compressor B faulted at 03:40. The backup compressor, Compressor A, tripped one minute later. The warning went to one on-call phone at 03:58 and was not answered. The backup contact acknowledged at 04:52, and Compressor A was reset by hand at 05:10. The highest air temperature in the log is -11.2 C.

18 pallets of the distributor's stock were quarantined at 07:45. We did not record how warm the product cores got, so we cannot yet say whether that stock stayed inside the distributor's limits. The alarm response was slow, and this report says so plainly below.

kpi Time above -18 C | 3 h 25 min | 04:05 to 07:30 | bad
kpi Highest air temp logged | -11.2 C | at 05:10 | neutral
kpi Pallets quarantined | 18 | about $286,400 at invoice value | neutral
kpi Cost so far | $9,850 | call-out, repairs, extra QA time | neutral

## What this means for the distributor's stock

- The distributor's stability data allows excursions up to -10 C for a maximum of 4 hours cumulative.
- On the air readings in the log, the freezer never got warmer than -11.2 C, which is colder than -10 C.
- That does not settle it. Product cores warm and cool more slowly than the air, and the log has no core reading between the start of the event and 09:00. At 09:00 a probe in the product core read -17.1 C, still above -18 C, 90 minutes after the air was back below it.
- The peak core temperature was not recorded. Until it is established, the 18 pallets (3-14 to 3-31) stay quarantined and the disposition is for the distributor's quality manager to decide with the evidence below.
- The $286,400 is the invoice value of the quarantined stock. It is not a confirmed loss.

The product in Freezer 3 belongs to three clients. The distributor's stock is the 18 pallets above.

